<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  NepalKit runs sandboxed (ADR-0008). The project set ENABLE_APP_SANDBOX = YES
  long before it shipped any entitlements, so the setting and the signature
  disagreed and the signed app carried no sandbox entitlement at all. This file
  is that decision, made concrete.

  network.client exists solely to serve the update check. Everything the app
  actually does — conversion, the calendar dataset, today's date, and settings —
  is local, so this entitlement buys nothing else.

  The two temporary-exception Mach-lookup entries are Sparkle's own services.
  A sandboxed Sparkle app must be able to reach them by name, and it cannot
  without these. `$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)` keeps them correct across a
  bundle-identifier change rather than hardcoding today's value.

  The Downloader XPC service is deliberately NOT bundled. Upstream documents the
  Downloader path's own costs: release notes fall back to a deprecated WebView
  because of a known WKWebView defect, release notes referencing external content
  stop working, and since 2.6 the service is not sandboxed by default anyway.
  Taking the entitlement above costs NepalKit nothing it wanted.

  This file is a release-contract artifact. Changing it after the first
  distributed build means re-signing, re-notarizing, and re-establishing the
  update baseline.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.temporary-exception.mach-lookup.global-name</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)-spks</string>
		<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)-spki</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
